 out differently he can't escape the  violent end that all of those present  will eventually know but nacho varga was  always different initially just because  he was one of a handful of characters  who don't appear in breaking bad but  there is much more to it in the first  season you're introduced to kim wexler  howard hamlin chuck mcgill and a variety  of side characters that all inhabit a  pre-heisenberg albuquerque about six  years before walter white is diagnosed  with cancer the problem with prequels is  you know how the story ends for the main  characters this makes for a great  situation for the unknowns and there  were big plans early on for nacho and  jimmy to be frequent antagonists then  there were some changes made to how he  would transform into soul and that  diminished nacho's role that's not to  say he didn't do anything important in  his introduction as one of tuko's more  reasonable associates he played a role  in saving jimmy's life only at that  point he seized on the information about  the kettleman's and tried to use that to  enrich himself he sort of faded into the  background from there and you wonder if  they named the third episode after him  more for the fact that his name fit the  convention of ending in an o rather than  how important he was to the story the  creators really liked the actor either  way so it was just a matter of time  until they found a way to utilize him  they realized that they could use his  uncertain ending to their advantage and  decided to make us like him that way if  they put him in danger there are stakes  in a way that there never can be for  characters like jimmy or mike this is  particularly effective since he's  surrounded by violent people but also  creates a challenge for making the  audience really care about him when  arturo died it was shocking because of  the method the way that it happened but  i think if you compare it to nachos  final moments you'll see how effective  this decision to make him different  turned out to be  it takes some time to develop though for  a while he continued to be a slightly  more palatable henchman compared to his  peers in the salamanca organization  right up until the moment he seeks  mike's help to make tuko go away he's  become erratic because of his using and  nacho worries that he'll come after him  if he doesn't make a move first mike's  code is stricter at this point in the  story and he doesn't want to kill anyone  this makes for an interesting  intersection of these two stories mike  is beginning his descent into this world  while nacho is starting to try to get  out and this adds to the gravity of the  situation they find themselves in later  he comes up with an alternate plan that  works but it also creates complications  and that brings tuko's uncle hector into  the equation things don't improve under  his new boss but he keeps his head down  until hector tries to use his father's  business as a front for their smuggling  operation his dad manuel varga is as  pure a character as this universe has  he's a normal citizen straight as can be  who works hard running his own small  business he's also a little proud and  very stubborn and because he doesn't  happily go along with the new plan don  hector doesn't trust him this makes  nacho fear for his life so he takes  drastic steps to try to take his boss  out the plan almost works and it would  have worked but gus intervenes because  he wants to decide how and when hector  will die this is all part of his quest  for revenge for the death of his partner  max and in the process of saving  hector's life he discovers what nacho  was trying to do which is information he  can use against him for his efforts of  trying to get out from under the  salamancas he's rewarded by being forced  to be a double agent for gus this has  him shot a couple of times to make up a  story to cover up what happened to  arturo things spiral when lalo enters  the picture to make things difficult for  gus who then resorts to threatening his  father's life and nacho realizes the  only way he can get out of this  situation is if he can get his father to  run away with him otherwise he'd never  be safe so by now it's clear that nacho  is no saint he made a choice to get  involved in the drug game in that he's  handed out beatings and even killed  people but where most people who found  themselves stuck in that position would  have just leaned in just went for the  money went for the power he went in the  other direction when faced with the  reality of who the people he worked for  were and of who he would become he  risked his life to become something else  and all of this made him sympathetic and  provided the story with a place where  tension can live because you just don't  know what's going to happen to him  unfortunately every failed attempt to  get out of the game left him in a more  compromised position he tried to set up  an escape plan that would include his  father and even tried to get mike's help  in relation to getting away from gus the  plan fell through when manuel refused  the offer on his shop because he figured  out that nacho was probably behind it  and probably using drug money and in the  wake of that they end up having a  conversation where he makes it clear  that he won't leave town i mentioned he  was stubborn and he lays it out there  that he won't accept anything short of  his son going to the police surely part  of this is him being naive not really  understanding what his son is up against  but there was another great decision in  the way they developed this in that they  made nacho the kind of character that  didn't want to put that burden on his  father he had so much respect that he  never gave him an ultimatum he wanted  him to decide for himself and when that  didn't happen nacho just tried to figure  out a different way to make it work he  won't be able to accomplish that and  this is where his story becomes  unflinchingly tragic he's forced to aid  gus in his failed attempt to take out  lalo because he could potentially expose  the chicken man's involvement both sides  want to kill him and it becomes clear  that he's down to just two choices he  could try to run knowing full well that  gus has already threatened his father or  he can forfeit his life to mislead the  salamancas pay the ultimate price not  for the mistakes he's made but for  something that would take suspicion off  of a person who's been torturing him  it's a hopeless situation but because of  the way the character's been developed  it makes complete sense that he would do  this in exchange for his father's safety  603 is nacho's episode it's his ending  and the name rockin hard place  encapsulates his story back against the  wall you see him strip down to his  essence and find out what's there a life  is decided through a series of choices  there's no control over how things play  out but in his time of judgment nacho  can choose who to be while many  characters would chase one last remote  or even irrational grab at survival  nacho varga chooses love for the one  pure thing left in his world a constant  in a chaotic unhinged existence like a  bright blue flower growing in the desert  it's not a perfect decision because  that's not how things work but it is a  perfect way to end his story  he's able to keep up his end of the  bargain by drawing suspicion away from  one tormentor and deliver a crushing  blow against the other there are no  guarantees only promises what matters in  the moment is that mike is the only  person he's grown to trust and he did  manage to end things on his own terms  i'd be remiss if i didn't mention  michael mando's masterful performance  again he brought the character to life  and his presence will be missed the  final phone call with his father will no  doubt be remembered as one of the most  heart-wrenching scenes in the series and  the adios papa just sticks with you and  i think that's a good place to leave  things let me know what you loved about  the character nacho varga and michael  mando's performance in the comments i  think the sheer impotence of hector  firing shots into his dead body is a  testament to what he accomplished and  i'd love to hear what you think please  like this video if you enjoyed it please  subscribe to my channel if you haven't  already and thanks for watching i'll  talk to you soon
 

